; MP/M page-zero definitions for uplm80
;
; Under CP/M these addresses are absolute and the compiler may emit them as
; literals.  Under MP/M a process gets its own memory segment: the CLI loads a
; .PRL at segment_bottom+0100H and page zero lives at segment_bottom, so every
; one of these has to be relocated by the segment's base page when the program
; is loaded.
;
; Only a *resolved symbol reference* reaches the .PRL relocation bitmap, so
; they are published from their own module: a reference from inside the module
; that defines them would be assembled as a literal and never relocated.  This
; is the same split DRI used - see PLM_WORK/X0100.ASM and X0200.ASM, which
; differ only in an `offset' equate so GENMOD's two-link diff would find them.

        .z80

BOOT    equ     0000H           ; Warm boot entry
IOBYTE  equ     0003H           ; I/O byte
CDISK   equ     0004H           ; Current disk
BDISK   equ     0004H           ; Current disk (alternate name)
BDOS    equ     0005H           ; BDOS/XDOS entry point
MAXB    equ     0006H           ; Top of memory address (in BDOS jump)
WBOOT   equ     0038H           ; Warm boot vector
FCB     equ     005CH           ; Default FCB (36 bytes)
FCB16   equ     006CH           ; Second FCB
BUFF    equ     0080H           ; DMA buffer (alternate name)
TBUFF   equ     0080H           ; Default DMA buffer (128 bytes)

; The command drive and the two password fields MP/M's CLI fills in for a
; transient: 0050H the drive the command came from, 0051H/0053H the address
; and length of the first file's password, 0054H/0056H the second's.  These
; are DRI's own values, from PLM_WORK/X0100.ASM.  MP/M II's CLI zeroes
; 0051H-0056H (NUCLEUS/CLI.ASM, "tptpw"/"tnpw"), so ED, TYPE and ERAQ -
; which test `len0 <> 0' - never take their password path.  With LEN0 at
; 0080H, the command tail's length, they took it whenever a file was named.
CMDRV   equ     0050H           ; Command drive
PASS0   equ     0051H           ; First password's address
LEN0    equ     0053H           ; First password's length
PASS1   equ     0054H           ; Second password's address
LEN1    equ     0056H           ; Second password's length

; The compiler's own page-zero references.  uplm80 emits these names, with its
; reserved `??' prefix, for the BDOS entry, the stack top and the warm-boot
; jump, so that a program declaring its own `bdos' or `boot' cannot capture
; them - UTIL7/DM.PLM declares a variable called `bdos'.
??BDOS  equ     0005H
??BOOT  equ     0000H
??MAXB  equ     0006H
        public  ??BDOS, ??BOOT, ??MAXB

        public  BOOT, WBOOT, BDOS
        public  IOBYTE, CDISK, BDISK, CMDRV
        public  MAXB, BUFF
        public  FCB, FCB16, TBUFF
        public  PASS0, LEN0, PASS1, LEN1

        end
